# ui/Components: pages, scrolling, widget state and data binding.

# Marks an entity as the root of a page subtree. The `id` is the
# stable page identifier (e.g. "now-playing", "album:beaches") used to
# scope nodeIds in `world.nodeIds` under a `"<id>/<nodeId>"` prefix so
# two resident pages can author the same nodeId without colliding.
# Descendants belong to the page implicitly via the Parent chain —
# walk up with `pageOf(uiSystems: uiSystems, world, entityId)` to find which page owns an
# entity. There should be exactly one PageRoot per page subtree.
# `presentation`: how presentPage shows it (ui/dialogSystem): full-screen,
# or a dialog (a card over a scrim). The one field that turns one into the other.
enum PagePresentation { page, dialog }

type PageRoot {
  id: String
  presentation: PagePresentation
}

# The ONE authored scroll component. Put it on the node that MOVES: the
# scrolled body of a page, or the Hug content inside a clipped viewport. The
# `scrollRootSystem` (ui/ScrollRootSystem) seeds its `ScrollState` on mount
# (restored from a `ScrollMemory` by page id) and the position is applied as a
# `RuntimeOffset` on the same entity — a transform-time translation, never a
# relayout. The scroll FLOOR (the most-negative position) is computed from the
# laid-out geometry, not authored: with `floor: content` it is how far the
# root's content — its own box, or the children that overflow it — extends past
# the window its parent shows (the smaller of the root's box and its parent's
# content box), minus `endInset`, the far-end length an overlay (a dock, a
# toolbar) covers. `floor: fixed` uses `fixedFloor` verbatim. A root whose
# content fits is inert: floor 0, nothing to scroll.
#
#     "Body": { "ScrollRoot": { "axis": "y", "floor": "content", "endInset": 390 } }
#
# Authored as `"axis": "y"` / `"x"` and `"floor": "content"` / `"fixed"`;
# `"anchor": "end"` holds the content's end in view (ui/ScrollAnchor).
enum ScrollAxis { y, x }

enum ScrollFloorMode { content, fixed }

type ScrollRoot {
  axis: ScrollAxis
  floor: ScrollFloorMode
  fixedFloor: Float
  endInset: Float
  anchor: ScrollAnchor = ScrollAnchor.start
}

# Per-root scroll position. Lives on the `ScrollRoot` entity, so a
# persistent UiWorld preserves scroll across page switches — the position
# survives as long as the entity does. `y` is the position along the root's
# axis (more negative = content shifted up / left; 0 = at rest at the start;
# it may overshoot the [floor, 0] range while rubber-banding); `velocity` is
# the momentum in points a second, retained so a fling continues across a dip.
# A virtual `ListView` host reads it too (ui/ListViewSystem).
type ScrollState {
  y: Float
  velocity: Float
}

# Stable widget metadata for data-oriented UI updates. These components
# intentionally carry app/backend-neutral data only: renderers do not
# read them directly, and app systems can query them to rebuild or patch
# dependent UI narrowly when data revisions change.
type WidgetId {
  id: String
  kind: String
}

type WidgetState {
  enabled: Bool = true
  selected: Bool
  loading: Bool
  error: Bool
}

enum DataResourceId {
  none
  albums
  history
  library
  playback
  playlists
  spotify
  settings
  debug
}

enum DataFieldId {
  none
  all
  isPlaying
  likedTrack
  playingTrack
  nowPlayingArt
  debugToggle
}

# Currently-playing cover art key — the now-playing / mini-player cover
# widgets observe this to re-point their Sprite.textureKey by identity.
type DataDependency {
  resource: DataResourceId
  field: DataFieldId
  observedRevision: Int
}

type ActionBinding {
  actionId: String
  role: String
}

type RefreshRegion {
  regionKey: String
  resource: DataResourceId
  observedRevision: Int
  dirty: Bool = true
}

# Declarative state -> visual mapping. Carries the visual variants a
# widget shows per WidgetState, so app systems flip WidgetState flags
# and `widgetStyleSystem` (ui/widget_style) applies the matching
# variant — no imperative per-widget sprite/text patching.
#
# Empty-string fields mean "leave that visual alone"; `hasTint` gates
# the tint pair the same way. `hasShapeFill` and `hasOffset` let the
# same state system drive simple shape toggles such as switch pills and
# knobs. `disabledAlpha` / `loadingAlpha` / `errorAlpha` are applied
# through the Opacity component for the matching WidgetState flags
# (1.0 = no dim).
#
# Theme references (#237, M6): each color pair may name a palette SLOT
# instead of carrying a baked RGBA. A non-empty `*Slot` wins over the
# literal and resolves against the active theme when the variant is
# applied (widget_style.rae), so a state color tracks light/dark like
# scene colors do (M5). Empty slot (the default) = use the literal —
# state-specific brand colors that map to no slot (e.g. the coral
# like-red) stay raw. `textStyle`/`textStyleSelected` were already
# style references, completing "reference theme, don't bake values".
type StateStyle {
  spriteKey: String
  spriteKeySelected: String
  tint: RgbaColor
  tintSelected: RgbaColor
  tintSlot: String
  tintSelectedSlot: String
  hasTint: Bool
  shapeFill: RgbaColor
  shapeFillSelected: RgbaColor
  shapeFillSlot: String
  shapeFillSelectedSlot: String
  hasShapeFill: Bool
  offset: Vec2
  offsetSelected: Vec2
  hasOffset: Bool
  textStyle: String
  textStyleSelected: String
  disabledAlpha: Float
  loadingAlpha: Float
  errorAlpha: Float
}
